```typescript
// TypeScript: caught before the program ever runs
function add(x: number, y: number): number {
  return x + y;
}
add("5", 3);
// error TS2345: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable
// to parameter of type 'number'.  -- never compiles
```

```python
# Python: runs fine until this exact line executes
def add(x, y):
    return x + y

add("5", 3)
# TypeError: can only concatenate str (not "int") to str
# -- raised only when this call actually runs
```

Same logical bug, same eventual failure category. The TypeScript version never produces a running program at all — you find out at build time, on your machine, before any user is affected. The Python version can ship, run correctly on every path that doesn't hit this exact call with these exact argument types, and fail for the first time in production, on whichever code path happens to trigger it first.
